<wpt lat="49.140100" lon="4.365800"><name>01 Mourmelon-le-Grand — Presidential Unit Citation (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>25 Feb – 1 Apr 1945

Back from Haguenau by boxcar. On 15 March Eisenhower reviewed the 101st and presented the first Presidential Unit Citation ever given to an entire division, for Bastogne. Replacements arrived; the veterans watched the Rhine crossings on the newsreels and wondered whether they would jump again.

Outside the map area: the Champagne garrison town near Reims. Included to open the sequence.

Accuracy: AREA — centre of a wider area.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.141350" lon="6.816500"><name>02 Stürzelberg / Dormagen — the Rhine bank (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>2–18 Apr 1945

The 101st was sent to the west bank of the Rhine south of Düsseldorf to face the Ruhr pocket. The 506th held the river line around Stürzelberg and Dormagen: outposts on the dike, a displaced-persons camp to guard, patrols by boat across the water. Winters' battalion HQ was in a village house on the river. Easy's first sight of Germany was a country of white flags.

The river dike at Stürzelberg is the reference; the exact billets are not recorded.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — close to the spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="50.941300" lon="6.958300"><name>03 Cologne — the ruined city (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>April 1945

Men of the 506th went into Cologne on passes: the cathedral standing in a sea of rubble, the Hohenzollern bridge in the river. For most it was the first German city they had seen and the first proof of what the bombing had done.

The cathedral square; the rebuilt bridge beside it.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — close to the spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.200000" lon="6.800000"><name>04 Ruhr pocket surrenders — the front dissolves (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>18 Apr 1945

The 317,000 Germans trapped in the Ruhr gave up on 18 April. The 101st's Rhine mission ended; the division was transferred to the Seventh Army for the drive into Bavaria and the supposed 'Alpine Redoubt'.

Area reference for the whole Rhine sector.

Accuracy: AREA — centre of a wider area.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.400000" lon="9.990000"><name>05 The autobahn south — Württemberg (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>22–26 Apr 1945

By truck and DUKW down the autobahn through the Rhineland and Württemberg, past columns of surrendering Germans and liberated slave labourers walking the other way. Ulm's cathedral was the landmark. Nobody shot at them.

Chronological reference only — the convoy route is not documented in detail.

Accuracy: AREA — centre of a wider area.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.038000" lon="10.725000"><name>06 Buchloe / Landsberg sector — into Bavaria (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>27–28 Apr 1945

The 506th came into the Landsberg area behind the 12th Armored and 4th Infantry Divisions. The country looked untouched; then the smell reached them.

Buchloe, west of Landsberg, is the reference for the regiment's approach.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — close to the spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.102800" lon="10.845200"><name>07 Kaufering — the camp Easy found (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>28–29 Apr 1945

Easy Company entered one of the Kaufering sub-camps of Dachau — usually identified as Kaufering IV at Hurlach, reached by the 12th Armored Division on 27 April, a day before the 101st arrived; the SS had burned the huts with the sick inside two days earlier. Typhus, starvation, the dead in the earth huts. Winters ordered food brought in; the medics then had to stop the men eating; Landsberg's citizens were marched out to bury the dead. Several Easy Company veterans later described the camp as one of their most disturbing experiences of the war.

Fields between Hurlach and Kaufering; a memorial stone stands where Camp IV was. The preserved earth huts are at Camp VII, south of Landsberg.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.030475" lon="10.852475"><name>08 Kaufering VII — European Holocaust Memorial (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>29–30 Apr 1945

The one Kaufering camp that survives: earth huts, foundations and the memorial stones of the nations whose people died here. 30,000 Jews passed through the eleven Kaufering camps building underground aircraft factories; 14,500 died.

Erpftinger Strasse, south of Landsberg. Place of remembrance.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.049000" lon="10.872000"><name>09 Landsberg am Lech — billets, and Hitler's prison (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>30 Apr – 2 May 1945

The company was billeted in the town where Hitler had written Mein Kampf in the fortress prison in 1924. On 30 April the news came that he was dead in Berlin. Sink set up in the town; the men were made to see the camp.

The old town on the Lech; the prison stands on the hill above.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.137200" lon="11.575500"><name>10 Munich — through the ruins (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>3 May 1945

The convoy passed through Munich, taken by the Seventh Army on 30 April, on the autobahn toward the Alps. The 'capital of the movement' was a shell.

Area reference: the autobahn ring, not the city centre.

Accuracy: AREA — centre of a wider area.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.724500" lon="12.877000"><name>11 Bad Reichenhall — the blown bridge (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>4 May 1945

The 506th's column was held up by a demolished bridge on the road into the Berchtesgaden valley while the US 3rd Infantry Division and the French 2nd Armoured got into the town first. Sink was not pleased.

The spa town at the mouth of the valley; the bridge site is on the main road east.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — close to the spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.626300" lon="12.992500"><name>12 Berchtesgaden — the Berchtesgadener Hof (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>5 May 1945

The 506th entered Berchtesgaden on the afternoon of 5 May. Sink took the Berchtesgadener Hof, the Nazi Party's grand hotel, as regimental HQ; Winters' 2nd Battalion set up there too, and the men found the wine cellars, the silver and the Mercedes limousines. The hotel was demolished in 2006; its site in the town, near today's Haus der Berge national-park centre, is several kilometres below the Obersalzberg.

Site only; the town centre and the old railway station (built for Hitler's guests) survive.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — close to the spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.633600" lon="13.041900"><name>13 Obersalzberg — the Berghof and Göring's house (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>5–7 May 1945

Hitler's mountain: the Berghof, bombed by the RAF on 25 April and burned by the SS, Göring's and Bormann's houses, the SS barracks, the tunnel system. Easy men walked through the ruins and helped themselves to what was left; Göring's wine cellar became legend. Most of the major Nazi structures were demolished in 1952 or removed in later phases; the Dokumentation Obersalzberg now stands on the site.

The Berghof foundations are a short walk from the Dokumentation centre; the bunkers are inside it.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.611400" lon="13.041800"><name>14 Kehlsteinhaus — the Eagle's Nest (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>5–6 May 1945

The tea-house on the Kehlstein, 1,834 m up, built for Hitler's 50th birthday. Winters, Nixon, Welsh and Easy men are said to have gone up after 5 May (who got there first is disputed); the photograph of them on a terrace, traditionally identified as the Eagle's Nest though some researchers place it at another Obersalzberg property, is the end of the Easy Company war in one frame. Whether they used the brass lift is uncertain — it had reportedly been disabled when the first troops arrived. The house survived because the RAF missed it.

Buses from Obersalzberg mid-May to October; the lift and the terrace are as they were.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.629600" lon="13.003000"><name>15 Berchtesgaden — VE Day (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>8 May 1945

The war in Europe ended with the 506th around Berchtesgaden. Winters' order for the day — 'effective immediately, all troops will stand fast' — and the champagne from Göring's cellar. The men began counting points.

Town centre.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.323100" lon="12.794000"><name>16 Zell am See — regimental HQ (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>9 May – Jun 1945

The 506th moved over the border into the Austrian Salzburg province to disarm the German forces there. Sink's regimental HQ was in the Grand Hotel on the lake at Zell am See; the town became the regiment's occupation capital for the summer.

The Grand Hotel still stands on the promontory in the lake.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.272300" lon="12.759000"><name>17 Kaprun — 2nd Battalion and Easy Company (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>May – Jun 1945

Easy was billeted in Kaprun, at the foot of the Glockner range, with the battalion HQ. Occupation duty: road-blocks, collecting weapons, guarding the vast dam construction site with its slave-labour camp, and a great deal of swimming, hunting and drinking. The Sergeant Grant shooting is usually placed at a road-block in this area; the exact spot, like the company's billets, is not documented.

The village and castle; the dams above are the post-war continuation of the Nazi-era project.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.427000" lon="12.848000"><name>18 Saalfelden — the German surrender (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>May 1945

Winters supervised the surrender of German units in the valley — columns marching in with their weapons, ordered to stack them and go home. Which formation and which commander is not recorded in a primary source. Tens of thousands of German soldiers were processed through the Saalfelden–Zell basin. Here, too, a German brain surgeon saved Sergeant Grant's life.

Market town north of Zell am See; area reference for the surrender fields.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — close to the spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.300000" lon="12.800000"><name>19 Zell am See — the summer of 1945 (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>Jun – 30 Jun 1945

Training for the Pacific began again in June — the 101st was scheduled to jump on Japan. Men with 85 points went home; the rest swam in the lake, drove Göring's cars and waited. On 1 July the regiment left Austria.

Area reference: the lake and the valley.

Accuracy: AREA — centre of a wider area.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.982500" lon="3.398600"><name>20 Joigny — the barracks in France (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>Jul – Nov 1945

The 101st was reassembled in the Yonne, at Joigny and Auxerre, to reorganise for the Pacific. Japan surrendered on 15 August and the jump never came. The company emptied by points through the autumn; Winters, Welsh, Speirs and the last of the Toccoa men went home one by one.

Outside the map area; included to close the sequence.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Germany &amp; Austria · Easy Company timeline</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.798200" lon="3.567500"><name>21 Auxerre — the 101st Airborne inactivated (Germany &amp; Austria)</name><desc>30 Nov 1945

On 30 November 1945 the 101st Airborne Division was inactivated in France and Easy Company ceased to exist. Of the 140 men who had jumped into Normandy, and the hundreds who followed, 48 Easy men had been killed by the Winters/Ambrose count — 49 by other rosters. The company's flag went home in a footlocker.

Outside the map area; the end of the story.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/germany-austria.html

